WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.927 --> 00:00:06.371
At the heart of every galaxy lies one of the most mysterious objects in the universe: 

00:00:06.371 --> 00:00:08.609
a supermassive black hole. 

00:00:11.382 --> 00:00:14.155
Millions to billions of times the mass of our sun, 

00:00:14.218 --> 00:00:16.834
these giants power astrophysical jets, 

00:00:16.834 --> 00:00:19.957
one of the most energetic processes known to physics.  

00:00:22.955 --> 00:00:27.810
In 2019, the Event Horizon Telescope released this picture of the heart of M87 — 

00:00:28.386 --> 00:00:30.438
the first-ever image of a black hole. 

00:00:31.421 --> 00:00:34.124
A second image, released in March 2021, 

00:00:34.319 --> 00:00:38.134
is helping astronomers understand the inner workings of astrophysical jets

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:40.072
and the celestial giants that fuel them.

00:00:45.428 --> 00:00:50.062
A quasar is a massive black hole that resides

00:00:50.303 --> 00:00:53.616
in the nucleus of most galaxies like our own.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.136
It's smaller than our solar system, and yet 

00:00:57.304 --> 00:01:01.007
 through a variety of mechanisms, it is able to outshine

00:01:01.723 --> 00:01:03.618
the whole galaxy around it

00:01:03.618 --> 00:01:06.116
so it is a very mighty engine

00:01:06.116 --> 00:01:08.116
that's very small, 

00:01:08.116 --> 00:01:09.216
but is very, very powerful.

00:01:09.216 --> 00:01:12.548
If you look at the essential features

00:01:12.548 --> 00:01:16.976
of what we currently think is going on  

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:19.456
in the tiny nuclei of these galaxies,

00:01:19.456 --> 00:01:23.301
is that firstly, there is a black hole there, which has this

00:01:23.301 --> 00:01:26.507
mass, but it has one other property,

00:01:26.507 --> 00:01:29.131
which I believe to be very important,

00:01:29.131 --> 00:01:31.396
which is that it is spinning.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.993
As a black hole spins, it draws charged particles,

00:01:34.993 --> 00:01:38.014
known as plasma, into its gravitational pull. 

00:01:38.673 --> 00:01:41.924
These particles create a magnetic field that ejects energy

00:01:41.924 --> 00:01:43.971
thousands of light-years into space.

00:01:46.737 --> 00:01:50.626
Jets themselves comprise very hot gas,

00:01:50.626 --> 00:01:56.877
and a magnetic field, which together move at a speed which is very close to the speed of light.

00:01:57.081 --> 00:02:00.176
Following the precepts of special relativity,

00:02:00.176 --> 00:02:05.385
that means that any radiation that they emit will be beamed

00:02:05.385 --> 00:02:07.639
  along their direction of motion.

00:02:08.855 --> 00:02:12.359
In 1918, Heber Curtis discovered the first jet,  

00:02:12.359 --> 00:02:15.668
which he described in his notes as a “curious, straight ray.”

00:02:16.668 --> 00:02:21.806
As it turned out, that jet belonged to the supermassive black hole in M87.

00:02:22.038 --> 00:02:25.495
So it's a rather special object in the life of an astronomer.

00:02:25.495 --> 00:02:31.361
I was a research student in Cambridge University  in the early 1970s.

00:02:31.361 --> 00:02:35.180
This was, in some sense, a golden time for black holes.

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:39.404
My friend and colleague Roman Znajek and I came up with a process

00:02:39.404 --> 00:02:44.709
which was able to extract energy from the spin of a black hole

00:02:44.709 --> 00:02:46.709
 with an efficiency of roughly 1/2,

00:02:47.739 --> 00:02:52.159
and then we said, well maybe this is the power source for the jets,

00:02:52.409 --> 00:02:53.670
and much else besides. 

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:56.887
The Blandford-Znajek process describes

00:02:56.887 --> 00:03:00.939
how a spinning black hole twists its magnetic fields into coils.

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:05.946
This motion creates a current of electrons and positrons 

00:03:05.946 --> 00:03:08.781
that flow out from the black hole in both directions,

00:03:08.781 --> 00:03:11.329
accelerated by the twisting magnetic fields.

00:03:12.647 --> 00:03:15.371
But until the recent Event Horizon Telescope image, 

00:03:15.371 --> 00:03:17.447
this process was just a theory.

00:03:18.125 --> 00:03:21.133
So what we see in our image is a spiral structure

00:03:21.133 --> 00:03:24.981
of polarization, which tells us that the magnetic field is also spiral.

00:03:24.981 --> 00:03:29.181
To have a Blandford-Znajek process, you need ordered magnetic fields 

00:03:29.181 --> 00:03:30.661
which is what we see.

00:03:30.661 --> 00:03:32.280
Our image is ordered. 

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:35.459
And the spiral structure tells us that there is a component

00:03:35.459 --> 00:03:38.000
in the magnetic field around the disk,

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.000
but there's also a vertical component.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:45.017
This means there's magnetic pressure that pushes out gas very close to the black hole.

00:03:45.838 --> 00:03:49.150
This is also what the Blandford-Znajek process predicts.

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:52.184
This vertical component, we think, is a direct link 

00:03:52.184 --> 00:03:54.945
to how the black hole is able to launch this jet.

00:03:55.947 --> 00:04:00.741
It is the most energetic, powerful process in our universe.

00:04:01.553 --> 00:04:03.544
These tiny nuclei,

00:04:03.544 --> 00:04:05.609
smaller than the solar system,

00:04:05.609 --> 00:04:08.463
but containing massive black holes,

00:04:08.463 --> 00:04:11.846
can produce as much energy over their lifetimes

00:04:11.846 --> 00:04:14.365
as the stars that surround them.

00:04:14.746 --> 00:04:17.934
And so when we ask, "How is the universe powered?"

00:04:17.934 --> 00:04:19.934
Yes, there's gravity. 

00:04:19.934 --> 00:04:21.934
There are stars.

00:04:21.934 --> 00:04:25.103
But these tiny but mighty galactic nuclei

00:04:25.103 --> 00:04:27.280
 are big players, too.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:30.320
And so they have a large role

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:33.703
in the growth of what we see in the universe around us.

